\section*{Irrationality of Catalan’s Constant}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 005; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that Catalan's constant is irrational.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
The claimed irrationality assertion is
\[
 G=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{(2n+1)^2}\notin\mathbb Q.
\]
This is Catalan's constant, defined by its absolutely convergent series.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 005; 2026-09-24), polynomial moment rows are arranged so that contact conditions eliminate the unwanted zeta value. A nonzero determinant is bounded at the real place and at finite primes, with a separate two-adic calculation; denominator clearing makes these bounds contradictory under rationality.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 005; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 005; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: Catalan's constant is irrational.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{Catalan.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.InternalCatalan.catalan\_irrational}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

